{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":155381,"duration_api_ms":161376,"ttft_ms":10900,"ttft_stream_ms":3937,"time_to_request_ms":15,"num_turns":15,"result":"```json\n{\n  \"outcome\": \"pass\",\n  \"reviewerLabels\": [\"epic-reviewer-1\", \"epic-reviewer-2\"],\n  \"reconciliationSummary\": \"Both independent reviewers returned pass with zero blocking findings, so there were no blockers to adjudicate. The reverifier independently re-verified every evidence citation behind reviewer-1's four non-blocking findings against the current code and artifacts: the fill-walk stop and chunk-candidacy predicate (select.ts:430-451, 471-485) plus coveredFrom as min over included entries' startOrder (select.ts:506-509) confirm E03-R1-001 mechanically, and the ruling artifact (artifacts/02-smart-compact/story-lead/001-ruling-response-001.json) exists and names the rejected 'smooth-obligate'/'partial-chunk' interpretations; cli/view.ts:20 and cli/index.ts:270 call domain surfaces directly while tech-design.md Flow 4 and the module matrix say the CLI builds an SDK, confirming E03-R1-002 as doc-vs-mechanism drift; thread-view/index.ts:403 stamps created_at with new Date().toISOString() while boundary.ts uses the injected clock, confirming E03-R1-003; openThreadDatabase runs migrations on every open (threads/internal/create.ts:191-205), confirming E03-R1-004. Architecture spot checks also confirmed reviewer-1's pass-supporting claims: the advance registers throw-isolated on ctx.onCommit ahead of the queue pokes (intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts:150-158) and never-backward is enforced both in the decision and in the UPDATE's WHERE position < ? clause (boundary.ts:145-152). The epic gate was re-run fresh by the reverifier: pnpm run verify-all exited 0 with 38 test files and 325 tests passing. Reviewer-2's coarser-grained pass contains no claim that conflicts with reviewer-1; its absence of non-blocking findings is a granularity difference, not a dispute, so reviewer-1's four evidence-supported non-blocking findings are retained as the canonical set. No new regressions surfaced and no scope was widened beyond the prior canonical findings.\",\n  \"crossStoryFindings\": [\n    \"No cross-story drift: Story 1's pull array shape is consumed unchanged by Story 2's snapshot serving, Story 4's boundary short-forms, and both Story 5 render targets; one assembly feeds both pull and materialize, making AC-5.3 parity structural.\",\n    \"All cross-story debts tracked across stories are closed: TC-2.5 view-health legs (Story 2), Story 2's sweep-receipt 'absent' literal replaced by SweepReceipt | { skipped: true } in Story 3 with no residue, AC-1.5's boundary-active leg (Story 4), TC-3.3's CLI execution leg and the CLI-intake-advances-boundary architecture-risk leg (Story 5 process suite).\",\n    \"select.ts prices band entries with the same renderer the band stores (renderArrangementEntry), so the tokens the fill walk budgets are exactly the tokens persisted — no drift between selection and rendering halves.\",\n    \"deterministicTruncation/ABBREVIATION_LIMIT in render.ts is a deliberate byte-identical restatement of turns/internal/compose.ts's truncateForFallback (turns domain frozen this epic; cross-domain internals may not be imported); documented duplication whose silent drift would change short forms — acceptable, on record.\",\n    \"Story 3's cross-epic hard gate was verified pre-launch against landed Epic 02 code; the sweep classifies on the persisted reason-class prefix, never on error prose (sweep.ts), matching the tech design's classification-table resolution.\"\n  ],\n  \"architectureFindings\": [\n    \"Module placement matches the tech design exactly: thread-view surface plus the seven named internals, the Story 0 seam, cli/view.ts, shared/view.ts; migration v6 matches the design schema verbatim (singleton CHECKs, band CHECK, FK cascade, view_boundary seeded to 0).\",\n    \"The sanctioned domain cycle (intake-stream → thread-view → messages/turns ← intake-stream) is explicitly annotated in scripts/check-boundaries.mjs, which pins the allowed edge set rather than asserting acyclicity, as the tech design required.\",\n    \"Flow 4 wiring verified at the source by the reverifier: the advance registers first on ctx.onCommit, throw-isolated so a failing advance cannot eat the queue pokes or reach intake's caller (intake-stream/internal/pipeline.ts:150-158); budgets resolve through the per-instance seam with below-SDK fallback to defaults (thread-view/index.ts:71-75); never-backward is enforced in both the decision and the UPDATE's WHERE clause (boundary.ts:145-152); the advance's zone sum and status's zone sum share one query, making AC-4.9's status visibility structural.\",\n    \"Must-not-own lines hold: the sweep touches only owners' report/requeue surfaces; status derivation counts go through reports; materialize renders pull output only; the boundary reads stored token estimates only; pull/status/materialize run under thread-touch suppression in both host modes.\",\n    \"Minor behavior-neutral deviation (E03-R1-002): the CLI calls domain surfaces directly instead of building an SDK as the tech design's Flow 4 prose and module matrix state, riding the design's own sanctioned below-SDK default-config path; the process-suite test proves CLI intake still advances the boundary end-to-end.\"\n  ],\n  \"epicCoverageAssessment\": [\n    \"38/38 ACs and 27/27 TCs are implemented and mapped to passing tests across view-fixture, view-pull, view-compact, view-select-golden, view-sweep, view-boundary, view-render-targets, and cli-process-view; critical TCs (TC-2.4 crash injection, TC-2.7 corruption refusal with control leg, TC-3.2 no-double-requeue, TC-4.3 monster-turn floor, TC-4.6 advance-failure with poke survival, TC-5.5 PI format conformance) were individually confirmed by reviewer-1 with file:line.\",\n    \"All eight architecture-risk tests from the test plan exist: zero-provider across all five ops, selection goldens G1-G4 plus the boundary trajectory as committed exact-value JSON, advance/poke seam isolation in both directions, restart-serves-snapshot via real-file reopen, coverage-edge accounting, CLI parity for all five commands, and CLI-intake-advances-boundary at the process boundary.\",\n    \"Fixture fidelity holds: all derived-form states are manufactured through real intake + drain against the scripted deterministic provider with no hand-written rows; the PI session fixture derives from a real PI session with committed provenance.\",\n    \"Epic gate re-run fresh by the reverifier: pnpm run verify-all exited 0 with 38 test files and 325 tests passing, matching both reviewers' gate claims.\",\n    \"One residual semantic edge remains, human-sanctioned by ruling 013 and recorded as non-blocking finding E03-R1-001 (straddling turns between smooth and detailed bands can go unrepresented without a gap entry while covered_from spans them); it is transient and the record retains everything.\"\n  ],\n  \"productionPathFindings\": [\n    \"No fake adapters, shims, or placeholders on any production path: no TODO/FIXME/not-implemented markers in src/; all Red-phase structured-result stubs are replaced by real implementations.\",\n    \"The deterministic provider is a test double selectable only by explicit name through the registry — no default, no silent fallback; no Epic 03 code path references providers at all, and the process suite strips LHC_PROVIDER to prove it structurally.\",\n    \"The Story 0 injection seam fires no-ops in production unless a hook is installed via the test-fixtures-only deep import; setViewInjectionHook is not publicly exported and the SDK's threadView surface carries exactly the five operations with no public boundary-advance.\",\n    \"thread-view contains no provider calls, no inference, and no polling or waiting; sweep requeues return at queue-row write; pull/status/materialize run under thread-touch suppression so they can never schedule background drains.\",\n    \"materialize writes only the caller-supplied output file and every generated field derives from view/record metadata with no write-time clocks, making byte-identical repeats structural (AC-5.2).\"\n  ],\n  \"blockingFindings\": [],\n  \"nonBlockingFindings\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E03-R1-001\",\n      \"severity\": \"minor\",\n      \"title\": \"Literal selection rules can leave straddling turns unrepresented between smooth and detailed bands\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Independently re-verified: chunk candidacy requires the chunk's newest live member to be strictly older than the oldest included smooth turn (select.ts:471-485, newestMember.turnOrder < oldestSmoothOrder) while the smooth fill stops at the first budget-crossing entry (fillBand, select.ts:430-451). Turns in the smooth remainder belonging to a straddling chunk — or closed-unchunked turns falling off the smooth share — get no band entry and no gap record, while coveredFrom is computed as the min over included entries' startOrder (select.ts:506-509) and can span them, so the omission is invisible in the receipt. The ruling artifact (artifacts/02-smart-compact/story-lead/001-ruling-response-001.json) exists and explicitly rejects the compensating 'smooth-obligate'/'partial-chunk' interpretations, requiring the literal rules — human-sanctioned behavior, transient (the next compact after chunking catches up re-covers the span), record-preserving (AC-2.9), but in tension with the epic's 'never silently omits a span' language; on record for the next design round.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/internal/select.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-2.4\", \"AC-2.5\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"human-ruling\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E03-R1-002\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"CLI wires view commands and intake to domain surfaces directly rather than building an SDK as the tech design sketch states\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Independently re-verified: tech design Flow 4 says 'The CLI builds a real SDK (cli/work.ts pattern)' and the module matrix lists cli/view.ts depending on 'sdk', but cli/view.ts imports the thread-view domain directly (src/cli/view.ts:20, with a header comment documenting the choice) and CLI intake calls intakeStream.messageEvents directly (src/cli/index.ts:270), relying on the design's own sanctioned below-SDK default-config fallback (thread-view/index.ts:71-75). Behavior is identical — no CLI view-config channel exists this epic, the operations are provider-free, and the process-suite leg proves CLI intake advances the boundary — so this is documentation-vs-mechanism drift, resolvable by a one-line tech-design correction or by wiring the CLI through createSdk later.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/cli/view.ts\", \"packages/lhc/src/cli/index.ts\", \"packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/03-thread-views-and-smart-compact/tech-design.md\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-4.9\", \"AC-5.5\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E03-R1-003\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"Compact stamps created_at from the real clock rather than the SDK's injected clock\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Independently re-verified: src/domains/thread-view/index.ts:403 uses new Date().toISOString() for the view's created_at while the boundary advance uses the injected clock (boundary.ts:152, clock().toISOString()). Determinism is unaffected — view_id derives from maxEventOrder and materialize's generated fields derive from the stored created_at — and the team dispositioned the same shape as accepted-risk for the Story 0 migration seed timestamp. Threading the instance clock through compact would close the small consistency gap.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/domains/thread-view/index.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-2.6\", \"AC-5.2\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"quick-fix\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"E03-R1-004\",\n      \"severity\": \"observation\",\n      \"title\": \"First pull/status on a pre-v6 thread file performs the one-time schema migration write\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Independently re-verified: openThreadDatabase brings a thread file to the current schema version on every open (src/domains/threads/internal/create.ts:191-205, runMigrations after validateThreadFile), so a pull or status against a file recorded under Epic 02's v5 performs the v6 migration before reading — a write on what AC-1.1 calls a no-writes path. This is the inherited Epic 01/02 migration-on-open convention, necessary for the view_boundary singleton to exist, idempotent, and one-time; recorded so the convention's interaction with the reads-only contract is an explicit acceptance rather than an unnoticed one.\",\n      \"affectedFiles\": [\"packages/lhc/src/domains/threads/internal/create.ts\"],\n      \"requirementIds\": [\"AC-1.1\", \"AC-2.8\"],\n      \"recommendedFixScope\": \"human-ruling\",\n      \"blocking\": false\n    }\n  ],\n  \"unresolvedItems\": [\n    \"Tech design open question Q1 (band messages split per-band vs one block) remains open by design; settle during PI extension integration (Epic 05/PRD-2).\",\n    \"Deferred items stand as recorded in the tech design: non-PI render formats; sweep cross-invocation requeue cap (revisit if repeated transient-failure loops appear in use); aging banded history out of the thread file (covered_from is the seam).\"\n  ],\n  \"gateResult\": \"pass\"\n}\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"156d739b-d79c-46f5-9211-46059e6b1d09","total_cost_usd":2.1961269999999997,"usage":{"input_tokens":2410,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60528,"cache_read_input_tokens":372826,"output_tokens":11652,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"